// ===================== 22.6 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('22.6  The page a stranger could check'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open, and the 18.4 page is required: line 4 read out, then what has changed in four units, then whether the **number** has moved. In most cases the number has moved and the student had not noticed, because nobody re-reads what they wrote in October. Where a student has lost the page, have them write line 4 again from memory and say so — the difference between the remembered line and the real one is itself the lesson.'));
out.push(A('**b** Belongs on a page for a stranger: **1**, **2**, **4**. Not: ✗ **3** a word of praise from your manager _(it is the ninth adjective in somebody else\'s mouth — but ⚠ a **dated factual sentence** somebody said, as in the model\'s_ "Nobody has ever asked me that"_, does belong)_, ✗ **5** a figure you cannot support, ✗ **6** the sentence somebody else **would** write about you _(nobody said it, so there is nothing to check)_.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **A** – the week-one note, four sentences in quotation marks with the date, at the top of the page; and she has taken off every adjective about her own character, _passionate_ among them.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** Because nobody reading the page will have any idea what four sentences cost unless she **shows them the four sentences** — and if she describes it instead, in a sentence beginning _I have made considerable progress_, they will have learnt nothing and she will have said nothing.   **2** The nine lines were for a **colleague**, who knows the work and supplies the context. A **stranger** owes you nothing and supplies nothing, so everything has to carry its own evidence, and the only sentence that will survive is one they could check **without asking a single question**.'));
out.push(NOTE('The count is the teaching device: **nine adjectives about your own character in one page is normal**, and a class that counts its own will not need the rule explained twice. The argument against them is not that they are untrue; it is that **not one of them can be checked and all of them will be on the other eleven applications**. Yara\'s replacement is the model: four figures with their sources, one refusal, and one real weakness. And the sentence to keep for the whole of Unit 24 is Ms Huda\'s distinction, because it is the reason this cycle exists four units after 18.4 — a colleague supplies the context and a stranger supplies nothing, so the same nine lines have to be rewritten rather than reused. Note finally what Yara does in the dialogue when she reaches for Jad\'s figure and stops herself: _no, that is Jad\'s figure, I have borrowed the habit of counting from him._ Borrowing a habit is the thing; borrowing the figure would have ended the page.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _reliable_ → _I have not missed a night shift since February, and the two I could not do I swapped myself, a week in advance._',
  '**2** _hard-working_ → _About a hundred and eighteen night shifts this year, out of a possible hundred and thirty-five._',
  '**3** _good with people_ → _About four night telephone calls a month that nobody else in the building can answer, two of them in English._',
  '**4** _organised_ → _Seven of my own errors are written in a notebook with the date against each, and five of the seven were on a fourth night._',
  '**5** _passionate about the work_ → _I ran a parallel check on lot 4471 for a fortnight, forty minutes a day, unpaid and nobody asked me._',
]));
out.push(A('Two checks on every replacement. First, **could a stranger go and find out?** — and _I swapped them myself, a week in advance_ passes because there is a rota. Second, is the figure **out of** something (22.3)? Item 2 is the model: a hundred and eighteen out of a possible hundred and thirty-five is an argument, and a hundred and eighteen on its own is a number. ⚠ And watch for the weakness that is a strength in a thin coat — _I am a perfectionist_, _I care too much_ — which is read as the tenth adjective and costs more than the nine.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
